Updates                                         RISC OS on Linux
=======
Configure's Updates keeps RISC OS on Linux up to date.

RISC OS on Linux      The parts of RISC OS on Linux itself, from the
                      riscos-rolpkg repository on GitHub:
  RISC OS             RISC OS (its ROM)
  Linux side          what runs RISC OS on Linux (screen, sound,
                      network, USB, Linux commands)
  Linux kernel        the kernel (on the card's FAT partition)
  Desktop parts       this plug-in, Linux network, the hard disc icon,
                      *Linux and USBDriver
Linux (Debian)        Debian's own updates, security fixes included.

  Check for updates   asks both repositories what's new.
  Install updates     installs everything new, keeping your settings
                      files. Restart (shut down and switch on) afterwards
                      to use a new RISC OS, Linux side or kernel.
  Roll back RISC OS   goes back to the RISC OS build before the one you
                      have (from the repository: it needs the network).
  Details             shows everything apt-get said.

These run as root, so they ask for Linux's root password (see *Help
LinuxPassword). Updating needs the network (Configure, Linux network).

If a new RISC OS won't start: put the card in any computer and create an
empty file called ROLLBACK on its small FAT partition (the one with the
kernel). The next start puts the RISC OS you had before back in place.
A new kernel keeps the old one on the FAT partition as *.old (for
example EFI/BOOT/BOOTAA64.EFI.old): rename it back if the new one won't
start.

